  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Round of showers arrives late tonight with \nthunderstorms possible Tuesday.\n\nSurface low will move east across the Great Lakes late tonight and \nTuesday, supporting a cold front which will pass southeast across \nthe region Tuesday evening. A leading shortwave trough, just ahead \nof the front, will support a round of showers late tonight and \nTuesday morning across the Niagara Frontier, likely extending east \ninto the North Country. With mid-level lapse rates approaching 7C/km \nand a presence of an EML and effective shear of 35 to 40 knot due to \na 50 knot jet at 700mb, a few thunderstorms can't be ruled out.\n\nWestern and north central New York will remain in the warm sector \nTuesday as the surface low passes by to the northwest. Lingering \nclouds and areas of morning precipitation will likely to inhibit \ndiurnal instability. However, where cloud breaks occur, the presence \nof dewpoints in the low-mid 60s will contribute to modest MLCAPE up \nto 500-750 J/kg in the afternoon. This could lead to the development \nof scattered thunderstorms. Favorable shear profiles will support at \nleast some low end risk for isolated severe thunderstorms, with \ndamaging wind gusts the primary threat. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Colder weather by the end of the week with some \npotential for snow. \n\nA deepening upper level trough will progress eastward into the Great \nLakes region Thursday night and Friday. The associated surface low \nwill strengthening across the Great Lakes with a sharp cold front \nsweeping through on Friday. There is still quite a bit of model\ndiscrepancies with this system and leads to varying timing and \nstrength of the overall system. Regardless, this set up will \nlikely bring the potential for strong winds Friday into \nSaturday. A much colder airmass will surge into the area behind \nthe frontal passage with pre-frontal synoptic rain showers \neventually giving way to lake effect rain and snow showers.",
